.TH PROFILELABTEST 1
.SH NAME
profilelabtest \- check every Danby radial structure model written as a profilelab(2) script
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B profilelabtest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Profilelabtest
reads the
.B SPEC
constant out of every model under
.B /appl/danby
that calls
.BR profilelab->run ,
so that what is checked is the string that ships, and then runs the march
that spec describes at its own parameters and at eleven pseudo-random
settings drawn from the sliders' own ranges.
.PP
No sample may be a NaN.  The enclosed mass may never fall as the march
goes outward, because a shell of non-negative density can only add to it.
And the normalised curve has to reach exactly 1 at the outermost sample,
since that is the value it was divided by.  A profile that fell as it
went out, or one whose normalisation quietly did nothing, would look
perfectly plausible on the screen.
.PP
.IR Profilelab (2)
does not insist on a
.B normalise
line, but every model in this family plots an enclosed mass against a
density fraction, and one that forgot would put an absolute quantity on
the same axis as a number that never leaves 0..1, so this test does
insist.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/profilelabtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR flightlabtest (1),
.IR poplabtest (1),
.IR profilelab (2)
.SH BUGS
The mass check assumes the normalised curve is the mass.  A model that
normalised the other curve instead would have its mass unchecked.
